what's a system of equations well  basically you know it's two equations  okay that represent lines okay generally  and we're looking for where they cross  the point of intersection now one way to  solve these really quickly on the test  is if they give you the points of  intersection you can just go ahead and  plug in those points so for example if  one of the possibilities the  multiple-choice answers was like  negative 1/2 you could say well I'm  gonna put negative 1 in for X I'm gonna  put 2 in for Y if that makes that  equation true and if I put it negative 1  in for X here and 2 and for Y and that  makes the second equation true if it  makes both the equations true we know  that's the common point of intersection  and that's our answer so you can back  substitute and that can be a very quick  and efficient way to do these types of  problems but sometimes that's not an  option so let me show you another way to  solve these problems so the first way  we're going to talk about it's the  substitution method so here what you  have is since y equals 2x plus 1 let's  go ahead and substitute or put that in  place of Y in the second equation this  way we'll get everything in terms of  just one variable X so let's go ahead  and do that so we've got 3x minus 2  instead of why I'm putting what y equals  which is 2x plus 1 equals negative 6 now  I'm going to distribute the negative 2  so that gives us 3x minus 4x minus 2  equals negative 6 combine like terms  that's negative 6 minus 2 equals  negative 6 add 2 to both sides multiply  by negative 1 or divide by negative 1  and you can see that x equals 4 now if  we want to find out what Y is we can  take X put it back into this equation or  this equation either one you'll get the  same answer I'm going to go ahead and  put it back in for X right here so  that's going to be 2 times 4 is 8 plus 1  is 9 so our answer is going to be 4  comma 9 now again if this was given as  one of the solutions you could go ahead  and back substitute to see if it makes  both equations true and you can verify  that that is the correct answer so  that's called the substitution method  the other method is called the  elimination method and what you do here  is you try to add the equations together  in such a manner that either the X's  cancel out or the Y's cancel out right  so for this one what I'm going to do is  I'm going to multiply everything in this  second equation by negative  so that this will be negative 2x when I  add it to positive 2x the x's are gonna  cancel one another out okay so you're  with me so far  so let's go ahead and multiply  everything here by negative two that  gives us negative 2x minus 2y equals  negative 14 I'm just going to bring that  top equation down below it okay like so  and if we go ahead and add these  together now you can see the X's are  cancelling one another out and we get  negative 5y equals negative 25 divide  everything by negative 5 and you can see  that y equals 5 now all we have to do is  take that 5 put it back in for y into  either one of these equations I'm going  to put it here because it's gonna be a  little bit easier and we've got X plus 5  equals 7 subtract 5 from both sides and  you can see that x equals 2 so our final  answer is going to be the cop the  coordinate 2 comma 5  now sometimes on the SAT it'll say you  know solve for X plus y and in which  case you would just add these together  you would say 2 plus 5 is 7 and that's  your answer or it'll just say what's the  the y-coordinate and you would just list  you know the Y value you don't have to  list both points so pay attention to the  format they give it to you in but  basically you can use a substitution  elimination or the back substitution  method to solve these systems of  equations so now here's your turn to get  some practice let's do these two  problems here go ahead and pause the  video to see if you can solve them on  your own we'll go through them together  the first one it says what is the value  of D such that the system has no  solution ok so when you look at these  two equations here you know what does  this D value have to be so that there's  no solution so go ahead and pause the  video see if you can do it on your own  we'll go through it together ok so let's  take a look so we talked about the  elimination here in this example let's  take a look at elimination for this one  as well what would we have to do - let's  just say eliminate the Y values here  well what we could do is we can multiply  the second equation by negative 2 that  would give us negative 10 X - 6 y equals  negative 24 right so now when we add  these together the first and third like  this we're going to get D minus 10x the  6y negative 6y are going to cancel and  we get 8 plus negative 24 which is  negative  sixteen now in order for there to be no  solution we want not just the y-values  to cancel out but also the X variables  to cancel out as well so if D okay was  this is actually supposed to be DX here  but if D was 10 you're gonna get 10 X  minus 10 x equals negative 16 which is 0  equals negative 16 which doesn't make  any sense it's a nonsensical answer and  what that means is that these two lines  are parallel and that they don't  intersect they don't cross okay so  that's the idea here now another way to  approach this problem is you could  rewrite the equation in y equals MX plus  B form that's the slope-intercept form  and as long as the two equations have  the same slope but different  y-intercepts what that represents is  they're going up at the same angle okay  but they're crossing the y-axis at a  different point those two lines also  will not have a common point of  intersection because they're parallel so  a couple different ways one way is to  use the elimination method and if both  of the variables cancel out such that  you get two quantities that don't equal  one another then there's no solution now  if we would have gotten 0 equals 0 or  negative 16 equals negative 16 then what  that means is that they're the exact  same line okay one line is right on top  of the other line and they're actually  crossing at an infinite number of points  okay let's look at example number two  see if you can pause the video do this  one on your own we'll go through it  together  it says if your friend has already read  150 pages and reads 20 pages an hour and  you have just started the book and you  read 30 pages an hour how long will it  take you to catch up to your friend okay  so see if you can solve this system of  equations to find out how long it's  going to take for you to catch up to  your friend in this particular book that  you're both reading okay so first thing  we want to do is we want to write the  equation so let's just say that your  friend okay is reading 20 pages and on  let's see 20 pages an hour right so 20  times the number of hours plus they've  already read 150 pages and that's going  to be the total number of pages that  they've read now notice that this  equation is very similar to the  y equals MX plus B form of a line the M  is the slope or the rate okay and the B  is the y-intercept or the initial  condition the starting amount how many  pages he's already read or she's already  read right now the second equation is  relating to how many pages that you read  and you read 30 pages per hour so if  it's 2 hours you would have read 60  pages 3 hours 90 pages but your initial  condition your B value is zero because  you haven't started the book yet right  okay so this is our system right here we  want to solve the system I think the  easiest way to do this is just to do  this substitution method so if P equals  30 H let's go ahead and put 30 H in  place of P so that's 30 H equals 20 H  plus 150 subtract 28 from both sides  right because you want to get the  variables on one side numbers on the  other divide everything by 10 to solve  for one H and you can see it's going to  be 15 hours so in 15 hours maybe not all  in the same day but after 15 hours of  reading you're going to catch up to  where your friend is at in the book so  this is a very important concept  understanding systems of equations and  the SAT they emphasize it quite a bit  in many different forms where you might  have to write the equations yourself and  solve the system they may give you the  equations and you have to solve the  system you might have to find a value  where there's an infinite number of  solutions or no solution or you know it  might just be like a you know some kind  of variation of what we talked about  here so review the lesson if you need to  I'll see you in the next lesson

